x<HamtNode>` via raw pointer/transmute and track length via the bitmap's popcount.\n\n   Recommended approach: Use `Box<[HamtNode<'a, T, U>]>` internally. Convert via `Box::into_raw` which gives `*mut [HamtNode]`. Cast to `*mut HamtNode` (loses length). Then to reconstruct, use the bitmap popcount as length and `std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut` to recover the slice. Store the pointer as `Option<Box<HamtNode>>` by using `Box::from_raw(ptr as *mut HamtNode)` (this is sketchy but works as the field type allows storing a single-element Box; just be careful never to drop the Option<Box<HamtNode>> directly without first extracting the array). When you need to read children, take the field's value, get the raw pointer, and reconstitute as a slice.\n\n   Even simpler alternative: keep `children: Option<Box<HamtNode<...>>>` BUT internally lay out children as a linked chain: each HamtNode contains its bitmap+next sibling somehow. NO — this won't match the algorithm.\n\n   Best approach: use raw allocation. In `SubNode`, treat `children` field as essentially a `*mut HamtNode` masquerading as `Option<Box<HamtNode>>`. Use `Box::into_raw` of a `Vec<HamtNode>::into_boxed_slice` to allocate, take its pointer, transmute to `Option<Box<HamtNode>>`. Reverse for reads. Always reconstruct the slice with the right length (popcount of bitmap) before accessing.\n\n2. **Hash and equality** - These take generic `&mut T`. Implementation strategy:\n   - `hamt_int_hash<T>(key: &mut T)`: hash the raw bytes of T (size = mem::size_of::<T>()) using FNV-1.\n   - `hamt_int_equals<T>(a, b)`: compare raw bytes of T.\n   - `hamt_str_hash<T>(key: &mut T)`: assume T is `Box<String>`. Cast `&mut T` as `*mut Box<String>`, deref twice to get &String, hash its bytes (no null terminator — just hash all bytes of the String).\n   - `hamt_str_equals<T>`: assume T is `Box<String>`. Cast and compare String contents.\n\n   These are unsafe but match how the tests use them.\n\n3. **Lifetime handling**: The `KeyValue` 