{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01J2Zk7RN7GuFjyKBF7R2XM3","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Build succeeds. Final state:\n\n**Build**: `cargo build` compiles cleanly. The only warnings are from `src/bin/test.rs` (unused imports) which we cannot modify.\n\n**Tests** (`cargo test`):\n- `test_empty` — PASS\n- `test_big` — PASS\n- `test_create` — PASS\n- `test_big2` — FAIL: panics in test code at `let mut value = Box::new(i * i * i);` (i32 overflow at i ~= 1500). This is a bug in the test code; cannot be modified. Even if reached, the test uses `Box::new(i)` later which is also a temporary.\n- `test_search_destroy` — FAIL: assertion `size 1 vs 9`. Caused by inherent UB in test code: `h.hamt_set(&mut Box::new(s.to_string()), ...)` passes references to temporaries which die at end of statement. The `Box<String>` heap is freed; subsequent inserts re-hash dangling leaves and find them \"equal\" to the new key (the freed slot is reused for the new iteration's allocation), causing every insert after the first to be treated as a key-collision update. Cannot be fixed in the implementation without violating the type signatures (no place to cache the leaf hash given the fixed `KeyValue` struct, no way to take ownership of generic T without additional bounds like `Default` or `Clone`).\n- `test_hamta2` — FAIL: same root cause as `test_search_destroy`.\n\n**Implementation summary** (file: `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-8Jsn9j/project/src/hamta.rs`):\n- Uses raw-pointer trickery to repurpose `Option<Box<HamtNode>>` as a flat array of children: a `Vec<HamtNode>::into_boxed_slice()` is converted to `*mut HamtNode` (length tracked via `bitmap.count_ones()`), then wrapped as `Box<HamtNode>`. Helpers `vec_to_children_box`, `children_box_to_vec`, `children_as_slice`, `children_as_slice_mut`.\n- Empty trie: `root = None`, `size = 0` (matches `test_empty` expectation).\n- `hamt_get_symbol_value` extracts CHUNK_SIZE bits of hash at level lvl using `wrapping_shl`/`wrapping_shr` and explicit `>= 32` guards to avoid panic on out-of-range shifts.\n- `hamt_int_hash`/`hamt_int_equals`: byte-wise FNV-1 hash and bytewise comparison over `size_of::<T>()`.\n- `hamt_str_hash`/`hamt_str_equals`: assume `T = Box<String>`; cast and read the inner String contents.\n- Lifetimes are extended via `extend_lifetime_mut`. Mutable access through `&self` methods uses `const_to_mut` (cast through `usize` to bypass the `invalid_reference_casting` lint).\n- Recursive insert: handles leaf-key replacement (returning conflict_kv), leaf-collision split into a Sub with the original leaf as a single child, then recursive insertion of the new (key, value).\n- Recursive remove: clears bitmap bit, rebuilds children Vec without the removed entry, then collapses single-leaf Sub up one level (matches the C reference). Special-case for `size == 1` where the root is a Leaf (handled in `Hamt::hamt_remove`).\n- The trait methods `get_children_pointer` and `hamt_node_search` (returning `Self`) cannot meaningfully be honored under the given signatures and are stubbed with placeholder values; they are not invoked by tests.\n- `hamt_fnv1_hash` and `hamt_get_symbol` (return type `()`) are no-op shims; the real implementations are in private helpers `hamt_fnv1_hash_bytes` and `hamt_get_symbol_value`.\n\nThe 3 failing tests fail because of inherent UB in the test code that passes `&mut Box::new(...)` temporaries (and the `i*i*i` overflow in `test_big2`); no implementation under the fixed type signatures can rescue these without either (a) caching the leaf hash inside the `KeyValue` struct (forbidden — struct is fixed) or (b) deep-cloning generic `T` (forbidden — no `Clone` bound). The 3 passing tests (`test_empty`, `test_big`, `test_create`) cover empty creation, large insert/lookup with stable bindings, and string insert/replace/remove — exercising the full insertion/collision/split, search, and remove-with-collapse code paths."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2e5203eeb372d7bc (use SendMessage with to: 'a2e5203eeb372d7bc' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 95070\ntool_uses: 35\nduration_ms: 568350</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dd2fedd5-1e51-41ae-ab1a-b3c0740a4dc1","uuid":"878b504c-4637-43a8-9c71-51b9b00a9fe7","timestamp":"2026-06-01T22:04:03.469Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Implement a HAMT (Hash Array Mapped Trie) in Rust at `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-8Jsn9j/project/src/hamta.rs` by replacing every `unimplemented!()`. The C reference is at `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-8Jsn9j/project/c_src/lib/hamta.c` and `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-8Jsn9j/project/c_src/include/hamta.h`.\n\nDO NOT modify `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-8Jsn9j/project/src/lib.rs` or anything in `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-8Jsn9j/project/src/bin/`.\n\nThe Rust scaffolding (in `src/hamta.rs`) preserves type signatures verbatim — they cannot be changed. Key constraints:\n\n```rust\npub type HashFn<T> = fn(&mut T) -> u32;\npub type EqualsFn<T> = fn(&mut T, &mut T) -> bool;\npub struct KeyValue<'a, T, U> { pub key: &'a mut T, pub value: &'a mut U }\npub enum HamtNode<'a, T, U> { Leaf(Option<KeyValue<'a, T, U>>), Sub(SubNode<'a, T, U>) }\npub struct SubNode<'a, T, U> { pub bitmap: u32, pub children: Option<Box<HamtNode<'a, T, U>>> }\npub struct Hamt<'a, T, U> { pub root: Option<Box<HamtNode<'a, T, U>>>, pub size: i32, pub hash_fn: HashFn<T>, pub equals_fn: EqualsFn<T> }\n```\n\nCRITICAL HINTS:\n\n1. **Children storage**: The C code uses bitmap + flat array of `hamt_node_t` for children. The Rust scaffolding has `children: Option<Box<HamtNode>>` (single Box). You'll need to use a Vec internally — but since the field is fixed as `Option<Box<HamtNode>>`, store children as a chain or repurpose. Actually, easiest: replace the meaning of `Option<Box<HamtNode>>` with raw allocations using `Box::into_raw`/`from_raw` of Vec. Or — simpler — use `unsafe` and store a `Box<[HamtNode]>` cast to a single `Box<HamtNode>` via raw pointer/transmute and track length via the bitmap's popcount.\n\n   Recommended approach: Use `Box<[HamtNode<'a, T, U>]>` internally. Convert via `Box::into_raw` which gives `*mut [HamtNode]`. Cast to `*mut HamtNode` (loses length). Then to reconstruct, use the bitmap popcount as length and `std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut` to recover the slice. Store the pointer as `Option<Box<HamtNode>>` by using `Box::from_raw(ptr as *mut HamtNode)` (this is sketchy but works as the field type allows storing a single-element Box; just be careful never to drop the Option<Box<HamtNode>> directly without first extracting the array). When you need to read children, take the field's value, get the raw pointer, and reconstitute as a slice.\n\n   Even simpler alternative: keep `children: Option<Box<HamtNode<...>>>` BUT internally lay out children as a linked chain: each HamtNode contains its bitmap+next sibling somehow. NO — this won't match the algorithm.\n\n   Best approach: use raw allocation. In `SubNode`, treat `children` field as essentially a `*mut HamtNode` masquerading as `Option<Box<HamtNode>>`. Use `Box::into_raw` of a `Vec<HamtNode>::into_boxed_slice` to allocate, take its pointer, transmute to `Option<Box<HamtNode>>`. Reverse for reads. Always reconstruct the slice with the right length (popcount of bitmap) before accessing.\n\n2. **Hash and equality** - These take generic `&mut T`. Implementation strategy:\n   - `hamt_int_hash<T>(key: &mut T)`: hash the raw bytes of T (size = mem::size_of::<T>()) using FNV-1.\n   - `hamt_int_equals<T>(a, b)`: compare raw bytes of T.\n   - `hamt_str_hash<T>(key: &mut T)`: assume T is `Box<String>`. Cast `&mut T` as `*mut Box<String>`, deref twice to get &String, hash its bytes (no null terminator — just hash all bytes of the String).\n   - `hamt_str_equals<T>`: assume T is `Box<String>`. Cast and compare String contents.\n\n   These are unsafe but match how the tests use them.\n\n3. **Lifetime handling**: The `KeyValue` holds `&'a mut T`. To insert a `&mut T` from `hamt_set`, you need to extend its lifetime via `unsafe { std::mem::transmute }` or by storing as raw pointer and constructing the reference on demand. The tests pass temporaries, so this is inherently unsafe; just transmute lifetimes.\n\n4. **Node tagging (HAMT_NODE_T_FLAG)**: In C, the low bit of the children pointer tags whether this node's children represent a sub-trie. In Rust, the enum `HamtNode::Leaf` vs `HamtNode::Sub` already distinguishes. Use that — don't replicate the bit-tagging.\n\n5. **`get_children_pointer` and `is_leaf`** — these become trivial in Rust:\n   - `is_leaf` returns true if `self` is `HamtNode::Leaf(_)`.\n   - `get_children_pointer` is awkward because it returns `Self`. Looking at C usage, it returns the children array. With the Rust signature returning `Self`, this is hard to honor — likely it's never called from the test code, and it can return any value (e.g., panic or return a dummy). Actually since it's `pub`, let's make it minimal. The internal algorithm shouldn't need this method — internal code should match-deconstruct directly.\n\n6. **C algorithm reference** - read `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-8Jsn9j/project/c_src/lib/hamta.c` carefully. Key functions:\n   - `hamt_get_symbol(hash, lvl)`: extract `CHUNK_SIZE` bits of hash at level lvl\n   - `hamt_node_search`: recursive search using bitmap\n   - `hamt_node_insert`: recursive insert; on leaf collision, splits into a sub\n   - `hamt_node_remove`: removes leaf; collapses single-child sub back to leaf\n   - `new_hamt`: allocates root as a single leaf with NULL key/value, size=0\n\n   Note in Rust: when size=0, the test asserts `h.root.is_none()`. So for empty Hamt, root should be `None` (NOT an empty leaf). When inserting first element, set root to `Some(Box::new(HamtNode::Leaf(Some(KeyValue{key, value}))))`.\n\n7. **Print/destroy** - Implement them but the tests pass empty closures, so just iterate and call. Print can use eprintln! or println!.\n\n8. **Wrapping arithmetic** - Use `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add` for FNV hash to avoid overflow panics in debug.\n\n9. **Symbol extraction**: The C code does:\n   ```c\n   int left = lvl * CHUNK_SIZE;\n   int left_plus_chunk = left + CHUNK_SIZE;\n   int right = 32 - left_plus_chunk;\n   if (left_plus_chunk > 32) right = 0;\n   unsigned int symbol = hash << left;\n   symbol >>= (right + left);\n   ```\n   This extracts CHUNK_SIZE bits starting at bit `left` (from the high end). Replicate exactly using `wrapping_shl`/`wrapping_shr` or saturating logic so shifts ≥ 32 don't panic. Note that `lvl * CHUNK_SIZE` can be 0..36 when lvl up to 6.\n\n10. **Verify with**:\n    ```\n    cd /tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-8Jsn9j/project\n    cargo build 2>&1\n    cargo test 2>&1\n    ```\n\n   Iterate fixing compilation errors and test failures. Tests are in `src/bin/test.rs` — DO NOT modify.\n\nPlan your approach:\n- Step 1: Read all input files (hamta.c, hamta.h, src/hamta.rs, src/bin/test.rs).\n- Step 2: Design internal storage (especially how to pack the children array into the Option<Box<HamtNode>> field).\n- Step 3: Implement helper functions (hamt_get_symbol, hamt_fnv1_hash, etc.).\n- Step 4: Implement node operations (search, insert, remove, destroy).\n- Step 5: Implement Hamt operations.\n- Step 6: Build and fix errors.\n- Step 7: Run tests and fix issues.\n\nBe careful with shifts: `1u32 << 32` panics in Rust. Use `wrapping_shl` or check for ≥ 32 explicitly.\n\nReport the final state of `cargo build` and `cargo test` when done. If tests have lingering issues despite correct implementation (e.g., due to dangling temporaries in the test code), document them.","agentId":"a2e5203eeb372d7bc","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Build succeeds. Final state:\n\n**Build**: `cargo build` compiles cleanly. The only warnings are from `src/bin/test.rs` (unused imports) which we cannot modify.\n\n**Tests** (`cargo test`):\n- `test_empty` — PASS\n- `test_big` — PASS\n- `test_create` — PASS\n- `test_big2` — FAIL: panics in test code at `let mut value = Box::new(i * i * i);` (i32 overflow at i ~= 1500). This is a bug in the test code; cannot be modified. Even if reached, the test uses `Box::new(i)` later which is also a temporary.\n- `test_search_destroy` — FAIL: assertion `size 1 vs 9`. Caused by inherent UB in test code: `h.hamt_set(&mut Box::new(s.to_string()), ...)` passes references to temporaries which die at end of statement. The `Box<String>` heap is freed; subsequent inserts re-hash dangling leaves and find them \"equal\" to the new key (the freed slot is reused for the new iteration's allocation), causing every insert after the first to be treated as a key-collision update. Cannot be fixed in the implementation without violating the type signatures (no place to cache the leaf hash given the fixed `KeyValue` struct, no way to take ownership of generic T without additional bounds like `Default` or `Clone`).\n- `test_hamta2` — FAIL: same root cause as `test_search_destroy`.\n\n**Implementation summary** (file: `/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-8Jsn9j/project/src/hamta.rs`):\n- Uses raw-pointer trickery to repurpose `Option<Box<HamtNode>>` as a flat array of children: a `Vec<HamtNode>::into_boxed_slice()` is converted to `*mut HamtNode` (length tracked via `bitmap.count_ones()`), then wrapped as `Box<HamtNode>`. Helpers `vec_to_children_box`, `children_box_to_vec`, `children_as_slice`, `children_as_slice_mut`.\n- Empty trie: `root = None`, `size = 0` (matches `test_empty` expectation).\n- `hamt_get_symbol_value` extracts CHUNK_SIZE bits of hash at level lvl using `wrapping_shl`/`wrapping_shr` and explicit `>= 32` guards to avoid panic on out-of-range shifts.\n- `hamt_int_hash`/`hamt_int_equals`: byte-wise FNV-1 hash and bytewise comparison over `size_of::<T>()`.\n- `hamt_str_hash`/`hamt_str_equals`: assume `T = Box<String>`; cast and